   row -- no regression (TestProbeX5EmptyKeySave).

DEFECT FOUND (reason for FAIL) -- SaveAlias's own documented contract is
violated in the collision + StableID-keyed-removal case:

  loader.go's new SaveAlias docstring states explicitly: "The key may be
  either a StableID or a legacy content hash." But when the caller-supplied
  key IS ALREADY the StableID form (key == hash, so canonicalKey is a
  no-op), the `if key != hash { delete(aliases, hash) }` guard that drops a
  coexisting legacy duplicate is skipped -- it only fires when
  canonicalKey actually changed the key. If a legacy hash-keyed duplicate
  for the same row also sits in the map (an anticipated, documented state --
  see rekeyToStable's own doc: "When both forms are present for one row the
  StableID entry wins"), a removal issued with the StableID
  (`SaveAlias(stableID, "")`) deletes only the StableID entry; the untouched
  legacy dup then survives into writeAliasesLocked, whose rekeyToStable pass
  resurrects it UNDER THE STABLE KEY on the very same write. Net effect: the
  removal is silently undone and the OLD alias value reappears.

  Reproduced with TestProbeX5RemovalByStableIDWithLegacyDupResurrects
  (written fresh in the copy, not part of either party's test files):
  starting state {hash: "Legacy", stableID: "Stable name"}, calling
  SaveAlias(stableID, "") leaves {stableID: "Legacy"} instead of {} —
    saved after removal = map[file:checking-2025.csv|2025-07-01|-3150|0:Legacy]
    --- FAIL: TestProbeX5RemovalByStableIDWithLegacyDupResurrects (0.00s)

  Scope note: the only current production caller (internal/handlers/explorer
  /handlers.go handleAlias, fed by web/templates/pages/explorer.html's
  data-hash="{{.Hash}}") always sends the legacy Hash form, never the
  StableID, so this exact path is not reachable through today's UI. It IS,
  however, reachable through the exported method exactly as that method's
  own new docstring says it should work ("the key may be either a StableID
  or a legacy content hash"), and the collision state it depends on is a
  first-class case the implementation itself claims to handle correctly
  elsewhere (read side, rekeyToStable's own doc comment). A future caller,
  a hand-migrated aliases.json, or a second writer that already speaks
  StableID would silently lose a user's alias removal. Per house rules
  ("default to FAIL on ambiguity" / oracle is narrower than the full
  documented contract), this is a real defect the Tier-3 oracle did not
  probe (its removal test only exercises the hash-keyed removal path) and
  is grounds for FAIL.

